@subsection Clipboard Sync

@cindex clipboard sync
@cindex kill-ring, Wayland sync

@code{cmacs-gowl-clipboard-sync-mode} provides bidirectional
clipboard synchronization between the Wayland compositor and the
Emacs kill-ring.

@defun cmacs-gowl-clipboard-sync-mode &optional arg
Global minor mode.  When enabled:
@itemize @bullet
@item
Text copied by Wayland clients is pushed to the kill-ring.
@item
Text killed in Emacs is placed on the Wayland clipboard.
@item
@kbd{C-y} checks the Wayland clipboard before the kill-ring.
@end itemize
@end defun

@noindent
Enable in your config:

@example
(cmacs-gowl-clipboard-sync-mode 1)
@end example

@noindent
The @code{GowlSeat} object emits two signals for clipboard
monitoring:

@table @code
@item clipboard-changed
Fired when a Wayland client sets the clipboard.
@item primary-selection-changed
Fired when a Wayland client sets the primary selection.
@end table

@noindent
These signals only fire for client-initiated changes, not when
Emacs sets the clipboard via @code{gowl-clipboard-set}, so no
infinite loop occurs.

@subsubsection Implementation notes

Clipboard reads from client-owned sources (including clients
embedded via @code{gowl-embed-app}) are asynchronous.  The signal
handler schedules an fd watch via
@code{gowl_seat_read_clipboard_async} and returns immediately; the
wl dispatch loop then flushes the @code{wl_data_source.send} event
to the owning client, the client writes to a pipe, and a
@code{WL_EVENT_READABLE} callback drains the data and hands it to
the Emacs main thread via @code{g_idle_add}.  A synchronous read
inside the signal handler would see EOF immediately because the
event hasn't been flushed to the client yet.

The sync @code{gowl-clipboard-get} DEFUN still exists for
peeking at the current clipboard state; it works reliably for
compositor-owned sources (text set via @code{gowl-clipboard-set})
and is used by @code{interprogram-paste-function}.

@noindent
Bar control:

@table @code
@item gowl-bar-height
Return the current bar height in pixels.
@item gowl-set-bar-height HEIGHT
Set the bar height to @var{HEIGHT} pixels.
@item gowl-bar-visible-p
Return non-nil if the bar is currently visible.
@item gowl-set-bar-visible VISIBLE
Show the bar if @var{VISIBLE} is non-nil, hide it otherwise.
@end table

@section Compositor Signals

@cindex signals, compositor
@cindex focus-changed signal
@cindex client-added signal
@cindex client-removed signal

The @code{GowlCompositor} GObject emits the following signals.
Connect to them using @code{cmacs-gi-signal-connect} or the
convenience wrappers in @file{cmacs-gowl.el}.

@table @code
@item client-added
Emitted when a new client maps.  The callback receives one argument:
the @code{GowlClient} GObject.
@item client-removed
Emitted when a client unmaps.  The callback receives one argument:
the @code{GowlClient} GObject.
@item focus-changed
Emitted when keyboard focus changes.  The callback receives one
argument: the newly focused @code{GowlClient}, or @code{nil} if no
client is focused.
@end table

@section Multi-Display Setup

@cindex multi-monitor
@cindex multi-display
@cindex monitor positioning

When running with @option{--gowl}, the compositor manages all
connected displays.  Use @code{gowl-set-monitor-position} to arrange
them to match your physical layout.

@table @code
@item gowl-list-monitors
List all monitor GObjects.
@item gowl-monitor-info @var{monitor}
Get full info alist (name, current-mode, scale, etc.).
@item gowl-monitor-position @var{monitor}
Get current position as @code{(x . y)}.
@item gowl-set-monitor-position @var{x} @var{y} @var{monitor}
Set monitor position in layout space.
@end table

@subsection Example: Dual External + Laptop

Two 2560x2880 monitors side-by-side at top, laptop centered below:

@example
(let ((monitors (gowl-list-monitors)))
  ;; External monitors side-by-side
  (gowl-set-monitor-position 0 0 (nth 1 monitors))
  (gowl-set-monitor-position 2560 0 (nth 2 monitors))
  ;; Laptop centered below
  (gowl-set-monitor-position 1501 2880 (nth 0 monitors)))
@end example

@noindent
Monitor connector names (e.g.@: @code{DP-11}) may differ from GNOME's
names (@code{DP-9}) since the Wayland backend creates new IDs.
Check @file{~/.config/monitors.xml} for GNOME's layout and match
by vendor/product.

@subsection Multiple Frames (One Per Monitor)

Emacs supports multiple frames sharing the same session.  In
@option{--gowl} mode, each frame is a separate Wayland client
that gowl tiles on its own monitor:

@example
(make-frame)   ;; creates a new frame on the next monitor
@end example

@noindent
Auto-create one frame per monitor:

@example
(when (> (gowl-monitor-count) 1)
  (dotimes (_ (1- (gowl-monitor-count)))
    (make-frame)))
@end example

@noindent
Each frame has its own buffers and window splits but shares the
same Emacs state.  This avoids the complexity of
@command{emacsclient} with @option{--daemon}, which is tricky in
@option{--gowl} mode because the compositor thread must survive
the daemon fork.

@subsection Testing Multi-Monitor in a Nested Session

When developing inside GNOME, simulate multiple monitors with:

@example
LIBDECOR_PLUGIN_DIR=/nonexistent WLR_WL_OUTPUTS=3 ./src/emacs --gowl
@end example

@noindent
This creates 3 undecorated gowl windows on GNOME, each acting as
a monitor.  @code{LIBDECOR_PLUGIN_DIR=/nonexistent} is required
because libdecor intercepts the first wlr_output and creates a
decorated window at 0x0 dimensions, which prevents the Emacs frame
from appearing.  Disabling libdecor plugins gives all outputs plain
undecorated windows that initialize correctly.

@subsubheading Known Limitations
@itemize
@item
Nested multi-output windows have no title bar or close button.
@item
The @samp{No free output buffer slot} warning from wlroots is
harmless and occurs during initial output setup.
@item
For production multi-monitor, launch from a TTY/login session
where gowl controls real DRM outputs directly.
@end itemize

@section Modules

@cindex gowl modules
@cindex modules, gowl

Gowl provides a module system for extending the compositor.  Modules
are shared-object plugins that implement compositor interfaces
(layout providers, gap providers, keybind handlers, etc.).

When running with @option{--gowl}, call @code{gowl-start} before
using any module functions.  The @option{--gowl} command-line flag
pre-starts the compositor early (before Elisp loads), but
@code{gowl-start} is still needed to load bundled modules and
initialize the Elisp integration layer.

@example
(when (and (fboundp 'gowl-running-p) (gowl-running-p))
  (gowl-start)
  (gowl-enable-module "alpha")
  (gowl-set-wallpaper "~/Pictures/wallpaper.png"))
@end example

@table @code
@item gowl-start
Initialize module loading and Elisp integration.  Safe to call
when the compositor is already running (idempotent).
@item gowl-enable-module @var{name}
Find and load a module by name, activate it, and dispatch startup.
@item gowl-disable-module @var{name}
Deactivate a loaded module.
@item gowl-configure-module @var{name} @var{alist}
Configure a module with an alist of string key-value pairs.
@item gowl-list-modules
Return a list of loaded module info alists.
@end table

@subsection Alpha (Window Opacity)
@cindex alpha, window opacity
@cindex transparency, windows

The alpha module controls per-client window opacity with automatic
focus dimming.  Unfocused windows fade to a configurable opacity.

@example
(gowl-enable-module "alpha")
(gowl-set-focused-alpha 0.9)
(gowl-set-unfocused-alpha 0.9)
;; Per-client override:
(gowl-set-client-alpha client 0.5)
;; Set all clients at once:
(gowl-set-all-alpha 0.8)
;; Query current config:
(gowl-alpha-info)
  @result{} ((focused-alpha . 0.9) (unfocused-alpha . 0.9))
@end example

@subsubheading Seeing the Wallpaper Through Emacs

To make the Emacs frame transparent so the gowl wallpaper is visible
behind it, two things are needed:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{PGTK @code{alpha-background}}: Makes Emacs render its
background with transparent pixels.  Text and UI stay fully opaque.
@item
@strong{Compositor-level alpha}: Any client opacity less than 1.0
forces wlroots to skip the opaque-region optimization and composite
the wallpaper behind the frame.
@end enumerate

wlroots resets scene buffer opacity to 1.0 on every surface commit.
Gowl re-applies the stored alpha after each commit so the
transparency persists across frames.

@example
;; In your Emacs config (e.g. Doom config.el):
(when (and (fboundp 'gowl-running-p) (gowl-running-p))
  (gowl-start)
  (gowl-enable-module "alpha")
  (gowl-set-focused-alpha 0.9)
  (gowl-set-unfocused-alpha 0.9)
  (gowl-set-wallpaper "~/Pictures/wallpaper.png")
  (set-frame-parameter nil 'alpha-background 85)
  (add-to-list 'default-frame-alist '(alpha-background . 85))
  (gowl-set-all-alpha 0.99))
@end example

@table @code
@item gowl-set-client-alpha @var{client} @var{alpha}
Set opacity of a specific client (0.0--1.0).
@item gowl-set-all-alpha @var{alpha}
Set opacity of all clients.  Returns the number affected.
@item gowl-set-focused-alpha @var{alpha}
Set opacity for the focused window.
@item gowl-set-unfocused-alpha @var{alpha}
Set opacity for unfocused windows.
@item gowl-alpha-info
Return the current alpha configuration as an alist.
@end table

@subsection Status Bar
@cindex bar, status
@cindex status bar

The bar module renders a compositor-level status bar at the top of the
screen.  It shows the focused window title and system info (battery,
clock).  The background is semi-transparent so the wallpaper shows
through.  The tiling layout automatically accounts for the bar height.

@example
(gowl-bar-enable)
(gowl-bar-configure
  '(("height" . "28")
    ("bg-color" . "#1e1e2ecc")
    ("fg-color" . "#cdd6f4")
    ("font" . "Hack Nerd Font Mono")
    ("font-size" . "13")))
@end example

@table @code
@item gowl-bar-enable
Load and activate the bar module.
@item gowl-bar-disable
Deactivate the bar and reclaim tiling space.
@item gowl-bar-configure @var{alist}
Configure bar appearance (height, colors, font).  An optional
@code{("position" . "top"|"bottom")} entry in @var{alist} selects
which bar slot the remaining settings apply to; if absent, the
top slot is targeted.  See @ref{Multiple Bars}.
@item gowl-bar-redraw
Force a full redraw of all bar surfaces (both slots).
@item gowl-bar-set-title @var{title} &optional @var{position}
Set the bar's displayed title.  Pass @code{nil} to revert to the
default (focused client title).  @var{position} is @code{'top} or
@code{'bottom} (default @code{'top}).  Use a polling timer to keep
it in sync with the active Emacs buffer:
@end table

@example
(run-with-timer 0.2 0.2
  (lambda ()
    (ignore-errors
      (gowl-bar-set-title
        (buffer-name (window-buffer (selected-window)))))))
@end example

@noindent
The right side shows configurable system widgets.  Default:
@code{cpu memory disk battery clock}.  Each widget can have its
own color:

@example
(gowl-bar-configure
  '(("widgets" . "cpu memory disk battery clock")
    ("cpu-color" . "#a6e3a1")
    ("memory-color" . "#89b4fa")
    ("disk-color" . "#f9e2af")
    ("battery-color" . "#94e2d5")
    ("clock-color" . "#cdd6f4")))
@end example

@noindent
The bar supports 25 widget types:

@table @code
@item cpu, load, memory, swap, temp, gpu
System monitoring: CPU %, load average, RAM, swap, temperature, GPU %.
@item disk, io
Storage: free space (with mount path), read/write throughput.
@item battery, uptime
Power and session: battery %, system uptime.
@item net, wifi, ip, vpn
Network: throughput (↓/↑), WiFi signal, local IP, VPN status.
@item volume, media
Audio: PipeWire volume (via @command{wpctl}), MPRIS now-playing
(via @command{playerctl}).
@item git, todo, podman
Development: branch + dirty count (from focused client's CWD),
Elisp-pushed TODO count, running Podman container count.
@item cmd:@var{COMMAND}
Custom command output with ANSI color support.  Colors apply
only to ASCII printable characters; Unicode (emoji) uses the
base widget color.  Leading @code{~/} in the command path is
expanded to @env{$HOME} at spawn time.
@item weather
Weather from @url{https://wttr.in} (15-minute cache).  Specify
location: @code{weather:London}.
@item host, user, keymap, clock
Session info: hostname, username, keyboard layout, date/time.
@end table

@subsubsection Per-widget refresh interval

Any widget can take a trailing @code{@@N} suffix to override its
refresh interval in seconds.  This works for every widget type, not
just @code{cmd}, and wins over the global @code{cmd-interval} config
key.

@example
(gowl-bar-configure
  '(("widgets" . "cpu@@1 memory disk:/@@60 cmd:~/bin/pomo@@1 weather:London@@900 clock")))
@end example

Setting a fast interval on one widget also speeds up the bar tick so
it can reach that rate (bar ticks at @code{min(2s, fastest @@N)},
floor 1s).  Slow intervals don't slow the tick down -- those widgets
simply use their cached output across redraws.

The last @code{@@} is the delimiter, so commands that legitimately
contain @code{@@} still parse: @code{cmd:ssh user@@host@@5} becomes
(@code{ssh user@@host}, interval 5).  If the text after the final
@code{@@} is not a positive integer, the whole token is treated as
the widget spec.

@noindent
Additional config keys:

@table @code
@item position
Which bar slot (@code{"top"} or @code{"bottom"}) the surrounding
settings apply to.  Defaults to @code{"top"} if absent --
existing single-bar configs keep working unchanged.  See
@ref{Multiple Bars}.
@item cmd-interval
Seconds between @code{cmd} widget re-runs (default 10).  This is
a global default; per-widget @code{@@N} overrides win.
@item widget-data-@var{KEY}
Push data for Elisp-driven widgets (e.g.@: @code{widget-data-todo}).
@end table

@subsubsection Multiple Bars
@anchor{Multiple Bars}
@cindex multiple bars
@cindex bottom bar

The bar module supports two independent slots per compositor: a
top bar and a bottom bar.  Each slot keeps its own widgets,
height, colors, font, title, and visibility.  The compositor
subtracts both bar heights from the monitor's usable area, so
tiling, vanitygaps, roundcorners, and @code{gowl-embed-app} all
respect both edges with no extra configuration.

The top slot is enabled by default with the standard widgets.
The bottom slot stays dormant until you configure it at least
once with a @code{"position"} key of @code{"bottom"}.

@example
;; Top bar (default slot)
(gowl-bar-configure
  '(("widgets" . "cpu memory disk battery clock")
    ("height"  . "28")))

;; Bottom bar
(gowl-bar-configure
  '(("position" . "bottom")
    ("widgets"  . "cmd:~/bin/scripts/tasks@@5 clock")
    ("height"   . "24")))
@end example

Convenience Elisp wrappers prepend the position key for you:

@example
(cmacs-gowl-bar-configure-top    '(("widgets" . "cpu clock")))
(cmacs-gowl-bar-configure-bottom '(("widgets" . "clock")))
@end example

Per-slot DEFUNs take an optional @var{position} symbol
(@code{'top} or @code{'bottom}, default @code{'top}):

@example
(gowl-set-bar-visible nil 'bottom)   ;; hide bottom bar
(gowl-set-bar-visible t   'bottom)   ;; show it again
(gowl-set-bar-height 32 'top)
(gowl-bar-height 'bottom)            @result{} 24
(gowl-bar-set-title "build: ok" 'bottom)
@end example

Hiding a slot releases its surfaces and reruns
@code{arrangelayers} so the tiling area grows immediately; the
slot's configuration is preserved across hide/show cycles.

@noindent
From the @command{cmacsgi} shell:

@example
cmacsgi bar configure bottom widgets="clock" height=24
cmacsgi bar configure top widgets="cpu memory disk battery clock"
cmacsgi bar height bottom
cmacsgi bar hide bottom
cmacsgi bar show bottom
cmacsgi bar title "hello" top
@end example

@noindent
From crispy or any embedded C scripting, call through
@code{cmacs_eval()}:

@example
cmacs_eval(api,
  "(gowl-bar-configure '((\"position\" . \"bottom\") "
  "                      (\"widgets\"  . \"clock\")))");
@end example

@subsection Rounded Corners
@cindex rounded corners
@cindex border radius

The roundcorners module replaces flat rectangular window borders
with cairo-rendered rounded rectangle frames.

@example
(gowl-enable-module "roundcorners")
(gowl-set-corner-radius 12)
(gowl-corner-radius)  @result{} 12
@end example

@defun gowl-set-corner-radius radius
Set the corner radius in pixels.  Requires the roundcorners module.
@end defun

@defun gowl-corner-radius
Return the current corner radius, or @code{nil} if the module is
not active.
@end defun

@noindent
YAML configuration:
@example
modules:
  roundcorners:
    enabled: true
    radius: 12
@end example

@noindent
The module implements @code{GowlClientDecorator}, replacing the
compositor's default 4-rect borders with a single
@code{wlr_scene_buffer} rendered by cairo.  Cached per client —
re-rendered only on resize or color change.

The decoration buffer installs a @code{point_accepts_input}
callback that always returns false, so pointer hit-testing skips
it entirely.  Clicks on the cairo stroke pass through to the
client surface underneath — no dead zones along the border ring.

Clients embedded via @code{gowl-embed-app} get special handling:
@code{gowl_compositor_position_embedded} calls
@code{gowl_compositor_refresh_client_decoration} after updating
the embed geometry, and the @code{gowl-set-client-border-width}
DEFUN does the same after changing @code{bw}.  When @code{bw}
transitions to 0 (as in the embed path), the decoration is
destroyed rather than left at stale dimensions.  This keeps the
rounded frame in sync with the embedded client's actual size.

@subsection Vanitygaps
@cindex gaps, window

The vanitygaps module adds configurable inner and outer gaps between
tiled windows.

@example
(gowl-enable-module "vanitygaps")
(gowl-set-gaps '(("inner-gap" . "16") ("outer-gap" . "8")))
(gowl-gaps-info)
  @result{} ((inner-h . 16) (inner-v . 16) (outer-h . 8) (outer-v . 8))
@end example

@table @code
@item gowl-set-gaps @var{alist}
Configure gap sizes.  Keys: @code{inner-gap}, @code{outer-gap},
@code{inner-h}, @code{inner-v}, @code{outer-h}, @code{outer-v}.
@item gowl-gaps-info
Return the current gap values as an alist.
@end table

@subsection Screenlock
@cindex screenlock
@cindex screen lock

The screenlock module provides full-screen session locking with PAM
authentication.  @code{gowl-lock} and @code{gowl-locked-p} are
available as C primitives; use @code{gowl-configure-screenlock} to
set colors, font, PAM service, and auto-lock timeout.

@example
(gowl-enable-module "screenlock")
(gowl-configure-screenlock
  '(("pam-service" . "gowl")
    ("auto-lock-timeout" . "300")
    ("bg-color" . "#1e1e2e")))
@end example

@subsection Scratchpad
@cindex scratchpad

Named scratchpad windows that can be toggled on and off.

@example
(gowl-enable-module "scratchpad")
(gowl-scratchpad-toggle "terminal")
@end example

@subsection Windowrules
@cindex windowrules
@cindex floating popups
@cindex modal dialogs

Applies @code{rules:} entries from the gowl config to every
newly mapped client.  Rules fire before the client is assigned
a monitor, so a matched rule can force floating, override tags
or monitor, and set an explicit geometry without the visible
tile@to{}float flicker caused by a post-placement transition.

Each rule supports glob or PCRE-regex matching on
@code{app-id} and @code{title}.  Regex mode catches apps that
update their title @emph{after} the initial @code{xdg_surface}
configure --- Zoom's "Join Meeting" window is the canonical
case and is covered by a default rule.

YAML schema:

@example
rules:
  - app-id: "pavucontrol"
    floating: true
  - title: "^Zoom Meeting$"
    floating: true
    regex: true
  - app-id: "org.keepassxc.KeePassXC"
    title: "Unlock"
    floating: true
    width: 500
    height: 200
    center: true
@end example

Float-management keybinds provided by the module (all
configurable under the @code{windowrules:} module config):

@multitable @columnfractions .35 .65
@headitem Key @tab Action
@item @code{Super+Space} @tab Toggle floating on focused client
@item @code{Super+c} @tab Center focused floater on output
@item @code{Super+Shift+Arrow} @tab Move focused floater
@item @code{Super+Ctrl+Arrow} @tab Resize focused floater
@end multitable

CMacs ships an opinionated default rule list covering Zoom,
Proton Mail Bridge, KeePassXC, pinentry, pavucontrol, GTK/KDE
file choosers and "Preferences" dialogs, and common image
viewers.  Override via the @code{cmacs-gowl-float-rules}
defcustom or the @code{rules:} YAML section; both surfaces are
additive.

@subsection Dropdown
@cindex dropdown
@cindex guake terminal
@cindex escape hatch

Guake-style toggleable windows.  Each dropdown entry has a
name, a @code{spawn-cmd}, a keybind, and a geometry spec
(anchor edge plus fractional or absolute dimensions).  On the
first keybind press the command is spawned and its first
Wayland toplevel is captured via a compositor prefloat hint.
Subsequent presses toggle visibility without destroying the
window, and re-compute the target geometry for the currently
focused output so the dropdown follows the user's focus across
monitors.

YAML schema:

@example
dropdowns:
  - name: term
    spawn-cmd: "foot"
    keybind: "Super+grave"
    width-pct: 1.0
    height-pct: 0.4
    anchor: top
  - name: btop
    spawn-cmd: "foot -e btop"
    keybind: "Super+F10"
    width-pct: 0.8
    height-pct: 0.6
    anchor: top
@end example

Because the module runs inside the gowl compositor rather than
inside Emacs, the dropdown keybind still fires even when Emacs
is frozen (native compilation stall, infinite Lisp loop, modal
prompt deadlock).  This is the primary motivation for the
feature: an always-available shell from which to @code{gdb}
the stuck editor.

From Elisp, the entries are configured via
@code{cmacs-gowl-dropdowns}.  Interactive commands:

@table @code
@item cmacs-gowl-dropdown-toggle NAME
Prompt for a dropdown name and toggle it.
@item cmacs-gowl-toggle-float
Toggle floating on the currently focused client.
@item cmacs-gowl-reapply-float-rules
Push @code{cmacs-gowl-float-rules} into the running config.
@item cmacs-gowl-reapply-dropdowns
Push @code{cmacs-gowl-dropdowns} into the running config and
refresh the dropdown module.
@end table

@section Elisp Convenience Layer

@cindex cmacs-gowl.el helpers

The Elisp layer in @file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-gowl.el} provides
