@section Screenshot and Recording

@cindex screenshot
@cindex screen recording
@cindex screen capture

The @code{screenshot} and @code{recording} gowl modules provide
screen capture with four selection modes: @code{desktop} (current
monitor), @code{window} (focused client), @code{area} (interactive
rubber-band selection), and @code{all} (all monitors stitched).

@subsection Screenshot

@defun gowl-screenshot &optional mode path no-clipboard
Take a screenshot in @var{mode} (@code{desktop} (default),
@code{window}, or @code{all}).  @var{path} defaults to
@file{~/Pictures/Screenshots/}.  Unless @var{no-clipboard} is
non-nil, the PNG is copied to the Wayland clipboard.  Returns the
saved file path.
@end defun

@defun gowl-screenshot-save-png width height data path
Save raw RGBA pixel @var{data} (a unibyte string) as a PNG file at
@var{path}.  Returns @code{t} on success.
@end defun

@defun gowl-screenshot-all
Capture all monitors stitched together.  Returns @code{(WIDTH HEIGHT
DATA)} where @var{DATA} is a unibyte string of RGBA pixel data.
@end defun

@subsection Recording

Requires @command{ffmpeg} installed on the system.

@defun gowl-record-start &optional mode path
Start continuous screen recording in @var{mode} (@code{desktop}
(default), @code{window}, or @code{all}).  The recording is encoded
by an @command{ffmpeg} subprocess.  @var{path} defaults to
@file{~/Videos/Recordings/}.  Returns the output file path.
@end defun

@defun gowl-record-stop
Stop the current recording.  Returns the output file path.
Blocks briefly (up to ~1 second) waiting for @command{ffmpeg} to
finalize the output file.
@end defun

@defun gowl-recording-p
Return non-nil if a recording is in progress.
@end defun

@noindent
The recording uses fragmented MP4 with @code{libx264 -preset
ultrafast -tune zerolatency -g 30}.  This produces output files
that are playable even if @command{ffmpeg} is terminated abruptly:
an empty moov atom is written at the start, and each keyframe
(every 1 second) flushes a complete moof/mdat fragment.

@noindent
Frame capture is driven by the compositor's @code{frame-rendered}
signal, which fires on the compositor dispatch thread after each
monitor frame render.  This guarantees that
@code{wlr_scene_output_build_state} runs on the thread that owns
the EGL context, avoiding @code{EGL_BAD_ACCESS} from cross-thread
renderer calls.

@noindent
Stopping a recording does a fast cleanup under the compositor
mutex (disconnect signal, close stdin pipe, send SIGINT), releases
the mutex, then waits for @command{ffmpeg} to exit outside the
mutex with escalating signals (SIGINT @tie{}→@tie{} SIGTERM
@tie{}→@tie{} SIGKILL).  The compositor dispatch thread is never
blocked for more than the fast cleanup.

@subsection Interactive Commands

@table @code
@item M-x cmacs-gowl-screenshot-desktop
Screenshot the current monitor.
@item M-x cmacs-gowl-screenshot-window
Screenshot the focused window.
@item M-x cmacs-gowl-screenshot-all
Screenshot all monitors stitched.
@item M-x cmacs-gowl-record-toggle
Start or stop recording.
@end table

@subsection GObject Types

@table @code
@item GowlCaptureMode
Registered GEnum: @code{desktop}, @code{window}, @code{area},
@code{all}.
@item GowlCaptureResult
Boxed type carrying pixel data, dimensions, file path, and
cancellation status.
@item GowlScreenshotProvider
GInterface for screenshot modules.
@item GowlRecordingProvider
GInterface for recording modules.  Provides @code{start},
@code{stop}, @code{is_recording}, and @code{finalize} methods.
The split between @code{stop} (fast, under compositor mutex) and
@code{finalize} (slow, outside mutex) avoids blocking the
compositor dispatch thread during child reaping.
@end table

@subsection Implementation Notes

The recording module went through several design iterations before
reaching the current working approach.  A few of the key issues
and their resolutions are captured here as guidance for future
modifications.

@table @emph
@item EGL context thread affinity
Frame capture must run on the compositor dispatch thread.  The
wlroots renderer's EGL context is bound to that thread, and any
call to @code{wlr_scene_output_build_state} from another thread
silently fails with @code{EGL_BAD_ACCESS}.  The recording module
connects to a @code{frame-rendered} signal on @code{GowlCompositor}
emitted from @code{on_monitor_frame}, ensuring captures happen on
the right thread.

@item Blocking vs.@: non-blocking pipe writes
Non-blocking writes of multi-megabyte frames produce short writes
that corrupt the rawvideo stream (ffmpeg reports
@code{Invalid buffer size}).  The module uses blocking writes so
each frame is either fully delivered or fails atomically.

@item Mutex deadlock on stop
Waiting for @command{ffmpeg} to exit while holding the compositor
mutex deadlocks the dispatch thread.  Stop is split into a fast
phase (disconnect signal, close pipe) under the mutex and a slow
phase (waitpid with escalating signals) outside it, via the
@code{GowlRecordingProvider.finalize} interface method.

@item Abrupt termination of @command{ffmpeg}
Other Emacs subprocesses (vterm @command{bash}, etc.) inherit
the ffmpeg stdin pipe's write end via fork, so closing the
module's copy does not cause EOF.  @command{ffmpeg} stays blocked
in @code{read()} and does not act on SIGINT until the syscall
returns.  The module's escalating-signal strategy (SIGINT
@tie{}→@tie{} SIGTERM @tie{}→@tie{} SIGKILL) combined with
fragmented MP4 output (@code{+frag_keyframe+empty_moov}) ensures
stop always returns within a second and the output file is
always playable.

@item libx264 lookahead buffer
libx264 buffers dozens of frames internally by default (lookahead
+ B-frames) and may not output any data for a short recording.
Using @code{-preset ultrafast -tune zerolatency} disables
lookahead and B-frames, so frames are emitted immediately and
the output file grows as soon as encoding starts.

@item Nested Wayland screenshot buffers
In nested Wayland mode the compositor's renderer allocates
GPU-only buffers that can't be read with
@code{wlr_buffer_begin_data_ptr_access}.  The
@code{gowl_compositor_screenshot_output} implementation falls
back to @code{wlr_texture_from_buffer} +
@code{wlr_texture_read_pixels} for GPU-side readback.

@item Repeated captures and damage tracking
@code{wlr_scene_output_build_state} skips rendering when there is
no damage since the last frame, which causes repeated screenshots
(including those used by recording) to return no buffer.  The
screenshot path calls @code{wlr_damage_ring_add_whole} before
each @code{build_state} to force a full re-render.
@end table

